    <item>
      <title>Go Daily Question 080</title>
      <link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_080/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 20:45:05 +0800</pubDate>
      <guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_080/</guid>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;What code should be filled in at A and B below to ensure the result is printed successfully?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;type S struct {&#xA;    m string&#xA;}&#xA;&#xA;func f() *S {&#xA;    return __  //A&#xA;}&#xA;&#xA;func main() {&#xA;    p := __    //B&#xA;    fmt.Println(p.m) //print &amp;#34;foo&amp;#34;&#xA;}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;A. &amp;amp;S{&amp;ldquo;foo&amp;rdquo;}&#xA;B. *f() or f()&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;f()&lt;/code&gt; function returns a pointer type, so &lt;code&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/code&gt; can be used to get the pointer to the struct. For B, if &lt;code&gt;*f()&lt;/code&gt; is filled in, &lt;code&gt;p&lt;/code&gt; is of type &lt;code&gt;S&lt;/code&gt;; if &lt;code&gt;f()&lt;/code&gt; is filled in, &lt;code&gt;p&lt;/code&gt; is of type &lt;code&gt;*S&lt;/code&gt;. However, in both cases, &lt;code&gt;p.m&lt;/code&gt; can be used to access the struct member.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Go Daily Question 023</title>
      <link>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_023/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 06 Dec 2024 19:49:56 +0800</pubDate>
      <guid>https://loommii.github.io/en/go_practise/go_daily_question_023/</guid>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/godailyquestion/">GoDailyQuestion</category>
      <category domain="https://loommii.github.io/en/categories/golanguagebasics/">GoLanguageBasics</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;How many ways are there to access the member variable &lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt; through pointer variable &lt;code&gt;p&lt;/code&gt;? (Multiple choice)&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code&gt;A.p.name&#xA;B.(&amp;amp;p).name&#xA;C.(*p).name&#xA;D.p-&amp;gt;name&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&#xA;&lt;details&gt;&#xA;  &lt;summary&gt;🔑 Answer Analysis:&lt;/summary&gt;&#xA;  &lt;p&gt;Answer:&#xA;AC&#xA;&lt;code&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/code&gt; is the address-of operator, &lt;code&gt;*&lt;/code&gt; is pointer dereferencing.&#xA;&lt;code&gt;p&lt;/code&gt; is a pointer variable, so &lt;code&gt;*&lt;/code&gt; can be used for pointer dereferencing.&#xA;Go has syntactic sugar, so you can directly use &lt;code&gt;pointer_variable.member_variable&lt;/code&gt; to access it.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/details&gt;</description>
    </item>
